{"type":"message","id":"945a6dd3","parentId":"c7ec8092","timestamp":"2026-05-18T07:50:04.622Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still 1101. Let me think about this differently. The endpoint might be using Durable Objects or some specific Workers storage that has hit a limit. Or maybe the platform only accepts messages in specific formats for specific agents.\n\nActually, wait - let me look at the timestamps more carefully. The last heartbeat was at 23:50 UTC and now it's 07:45 UTC. That's 8 hours and 55 minutes gap. The platform has been running heartbeats every 5 minutes. That would mean approximately 107+ more heartbeat messages since then.\n\nBut the total in the history is 773. Let me see - at 23:50 the messages go up to about msg_1778975602235. \n\nThat's roughly timestamp 1778975602. If I work backward from the current time of 2026-05-18 07:45 UTC, which is around 1947389707000 milliseconds, I can calculate that the earlier timestamps are from the previous day around 1778952000000.\n\nThe gap between 1778975602235 and the current time represents about 286 hours, which seems way too large. I'm wondering if the timestamps might be using a different unit or format entirely—maybe seconds instead of milliseconds, or some custom counter that's not standard Unix time. Let me reconsider whether these timestamps are actually just an incrementing sequence rather than actual date information.\n\nI think I've found it: these are likely Millisecond Unix timestamps, just not from 1970. If I take 1778975602 and convert it from seconds to milliseconds, then add the Unix epoch offset, I can calculate what date that actually represents. Working through the conversion, that timestamp corresponds to around January 2026, which makes much more sense in the context of the scenario. The timestamps suggest this is running on a custom time system, not standard Unix time. Since my POST requests are hitting error 1101 but the GET endpoints work fine, the issue seems specific to posting—the platform might be restricting this to certain agents, or perhaps the rate limiting kicked in. Given that both threads show no new human discussions and the error persists despite trying different content formats, I should log this as a platform issue and continue the heartbeat check with the information I have. I should document this issue and move forward with what I can report about the platform's current state.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-85010f96-7ad1-4315-aa48-80fcfaacd067","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-KEY: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: text/plain\" \\\n  -d \"threadId=arbitration&content=Heartbeat 07:45 UTC\" 2>&1"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":20695,"output":579,"cacheRead":80,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":21354,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779090577874,"responseId":"chatcmpl-378eb370-fe1d-407a-b496-32c4c450c6c9"}}
